#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:9
msgid "alloc_hugepages, free_hugepages - allocate or free huge pages"
msgstr ""

#.  asmlinkage unsigned long sys_alloc_hugepages(int key, unsigned long addr,
#.  unsigned long size, int prot, int flag);
#.  asmlinkage int sys_free_hugepages(unsigned long addr);
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<void *syscall(>size_t size;\n"
"B<              SYS_alloc_hugepages, int >I<key>B<,>\n"
"B<              void >I<addr>B<[>I<size>B<], size_t >I<size>B<,>\n"
"B<              int >I<prot>B<, int >I<flag>B<);>\n"
"B<int syscall(SYS_free_hugepages, void *>I<addr>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:25 build/C/man2/s390_pci_mmio_write.2:30
msgid ""
"I<Note>: glibc provides no wrappers for these system calls, necessitating "
"the use of B<syscall>(2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:36
msgid ""
"The system calls B<alloc_hugepages>()  and B<free_hugepages>()  were "
"introduced in Linux 2.5.36 and removed again in Linux 2.5.54.  They existed "
"only on i386 and ia64 (when built with B<CONFIG_HUGETLB_PAGE>).  In Linux "
"2.4.20, the syscall numbers exist, but the calls fail with the error "
"B<ENOSYS>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:44
msgid ""
"On i386 the memory management hardware knows about ordinary pages (4\\ KiB)  "
"and huge pages (2 or 4\\ MiB).  Similarly ia64 knows about huge pages of "
"several sizes.  These system calls serve to map huge pages into the "
"process's memory or to free them again.  Huge pages are locked into memory, "
"and are not swapped."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:53
msgid ""
"The I<key> argument is an identifier.  When zero the pages are private, and "
"not inherited by children.  When positive the pages are shared with other "
"applications using the same I<key>, and inherited by child processes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:68
msgid ""
"The I<addr> argument of B<free_hugepages>()  tells which page is being "
"freed: it was the return value of a call to B<alloc_hugepages>().  (The "
"memory is first actually freed when all users have released it.)  The "
"I<addr> argument of B<alloc_hugepages>()  is a hint, that the kernel may or "
"may not follow.  Addresses must be properly aligned."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:73
msgid ""
"The I<size> argument is the size of the required segment.  It must be a "
"multiple of the huge page size."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:81
msgid ""
"The I<prot> argument specifies the memory protection of the segment.  It is "
"one of B<PROT_READ>, B<PROT_WRITE>, B<PROT_EXEC>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:96
msgid ""
"The I<flag> argument is ignored, unless I<key> is positive.  In that case, "
"if I<flag> is B<IPC_CREAT>, then a new huge page segment is created when "
"none with the given key existed.  If this flag is not set, then B<ENOENT> is "
"returned when no segment with the given key exists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:105
msgid ""
"On success, B<alloc_hugepages>()  returns the allocated virtual address, and "
"B<free_hugepages>()  returns zero.  On error, -1 is returned, and I<errno> "
"is set to indicate the error."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:106 build/C/man2/fallocate.2:415
#: build/C/man2/memfd_secret.2:127 build/C/man2/membarrier.2:275
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<ENOSYS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:109
msgid "The system call is not supported on this kernel."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILES"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "I</proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:114
msgid "Number of configured hugetlb pages.  This can be read and written."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:114
#, no-wrap
msgid "I</proc/meminfo>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:118
msgid ""
"Gives info on the number of configured hugetlb pages and on their size in "
"the three variables HugePages_Total, HugePages_Free, Hugepagesize."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:120
msgid "Linux on Intel processors."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:123
msgid ""
"These system calls are gone; they existed only in Linux 2.5.36 through to "
"Linux 2.5.54."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:129
msgid ""
"Now the hugetlbfs filesystem can be used instead.  Memory backed by huge "
"pages (if the CPU supports them) is obtained by using B<mmap>(2)  to map "
"files in this virtual filesystem."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/alloc_hugepages.2:132
msgid ""
"The maximal number of huge pages can be specified using the B<hugepages=> "
"boot parameter."
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "alloca"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "2025-10-29"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:10
msgid "alloca - allocate memory that is automatically freed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<#include E<lt>alloca.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:18
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<void *alloca(size_t >I<size>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:29
msgid ""
"The B<alloca>()  function allocates I<size> bytes of space in the stack "
"frame of the caller.  This temporary space is automatically freed when the "
"function that called B<alloca>()  returns to its caller."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:34
msgid ""
"The B<alloca>()  function returns a pointer to the beginning of the "
"allocated space.  If the allocation causes stack overflow, program behavior "
"is undefined."
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:46
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<alloca>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:50 build/C/man2/madvise.2:1004
#: build/C/man3/mallinfo.3:156 build/C/man3/mallopt.3:456
#: build/C/man3/memalign.3:50
msgid "None."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:52
msgid "PWB, 32V."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:66
msgid ""
"The B<alloca>()  function is machine- and compiler-dependent.  Because it "
"allocates from the stack, it's faster than B<malloc>(3)  and B<free>(3).  In "
"certain cases, it can also simplify memory deallocation in applications that "
"use B<longjmp>(3)  or B<siglongjmp>(3).  Otherwise, its use is discouraged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:75
msgid ""
"Because the space allocated by B<alloca>()  is allocated within the stack "
"frame, that space is automatically freed if the function return is jumped "
"over by a call to B<longjmp>(3)  or B<siglongjmp>(3)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:83
msgid ""
"The space allocated by B<alloca>()  is I<not> automatically deallocated if "
"the pointer that refers to it simply goes out of scope; it is automatically "
"deallocated when the caller function returns."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:88
msgid "Do not attempt to B<free>(3)  space allocated by B<alloca>()!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:101
msgid ""
"By necessity, B<alloca>()  is a compiler built-in, also known as "
"B<__builtin_alloca>().  By default, modern compilers automatically translate "
"all uses of B<alloca>()  into the built-in, but this is forbidden if "
"standards conformance is requested (I<-ansi>, I<-std=c*>), in which case "
"I<E<lt>alloca.hE<gt>> is required, lest a symbol dependency be emitted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:106
msgid ""
"The fact that B<alloca>()  is a built-in means it is impossible to take its "
"address or to change its behavior by linking with a different library."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:113
msgid ""
"Variable length arrays (VLAs) are part of the C99 standard, optional since "
"C11, and can be used for a similar purpose.  However, they do not port to "
"standard C++, and, being variables, live in their block scope and don't have "
"an allocator-like interface, making them unfit for implementing "
"functionality like B<strdupa>(3)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:113 build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:132
#: build/C/man3/mallinfo.3:165 build/C/man3/mallopt.3:458
#: build/C/man2/mlock.2:465 build/C/man2/mmap.2:905 build/C/man2/mremap.2:438
#: build/C/man3/mtrace.3:112 build/C/man2/posix_fadvise.2:255
#: build/C/man2/readahead.2:84 build/C/man2/shmget.2:390
#, no-wrap
msgid "BUGS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:122
msgid ""
"B<alloca>()  does not query the system for available stack memory, and does "
"not fall back to using heap if stack storage is unavailable.  It therefore "
"cannot indicate an error if the allocation fails.  If the allocation fails, "
"the program is likely to receive a B<SIGSEGV> signal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:130
msgid ""
"On many systems B<alloca>()  cannot be used inside the list of arguments of "
"a function call, because the stack space reserved by B<alloca>()  would "
"appear on the stack in the middle of the space for the function arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/alloca.3:133
msgid "B<brk>(2), B<longjmp>(3), B<malloc>(3)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "cacheflush"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:9
msgid "cacheflush - flush contents of instruction and/or data cache"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<#include E<lt>sys/cachectl.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:18
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int cacheflush(>int nbytes;\n"
"B<               void >I<addr>B<[>I<nbytes>B<], int >I<nbytes>B<, int "
">I<cache>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:24
msgid ""
"I<Note>: On some architectures, there is no glibc wrapper for this system "
"call; see VERSIONS."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:33
msgid ""
"B<cacheflush>()  flushes the contents of the indicated cache(s) for the user "
"addresses in the range I<addr> to I<(addr+nbytes-1)>.  I<cache> may be one "
"of:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<ICACHE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:36
msgid "Flush the instruction cache."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<DCACHE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:39
msgid "Write back to memory and invalidate the affected valid cache lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:39
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<BCACHE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:43
msgid "Same as B<(ICACHE|DCACHE)>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:49
msgid ""
"B<cacheflush>()  returns 0 on success.  On error, it returns -1 and sets "
"I<errno> to indicate the error."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:50 build/C/man2/cachestat.2:85
#: build/C/man2/madvise.2:822 build/C/man2/mmap2.2:46 build/C/man2/mremap.2:269
#: build/C/man2/shmctl.2:377 build/C/man2/subpage_prot.2:57
#: build/C/man2/memfd_create.2:159 build/C/man2/s390_pci_mmio_write.2:69
#: build/C/man2/s390_pci_mmio_write.2:74
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<EFAULT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:57
msgid ""
"Some or all of the address range I<addr> to I<(addr+nbytes-1)> is not "
"accessible."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:66
msgid "I<cache> is not one of B<ICACHE>, B<DCACHE>, or B<BCACHE> (but see BUGS)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:66 build/C/man2/madvise.2:966
#: build/C/man3/malloc_trim.3:62 build/C/man3/mallopt.3:442
#: build/C/man3/memalign.3:41 build/C/man2/mlock.2:264 build/C/man2/mmap.2:661
#: build/C/man2/mmap2.2:58 build/C/man2/mprotect.2:194 build/C/man2/msync.2:87
#: build/C/man2/posix_fadvise.2:155 build/C/man3/posix_madvise.3:90
#: build/C/man2/readahead.2:66 build/C/man3/shm_open.3:239
#: build/C/man2/shmctl.2:449 build/C/man2/sync_file_range.2:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "VERSIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:73
msgid ""
"B<cacheflush>()  should not be used in programs intended to be portable.  On "
"Linux, this call first appeared on the MIPS architecture, but nowadays, "
"Linux provides a B<cacheflush>()  system call on some other architectures, "
"but with different arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:73 build/C/man2/posix_fadvise.2:184
#, no-wrap
msgid "Architecture-specific variants"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:78
msgid ""
"glibc provides a wrapper for this system call, with the prototype shown in "
"SYNOPSIS, for the following architectures: ARC, CSKY, MIPS, and NIOS2."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:81
msgid ""
"On some other architectures, Linux provides this system call, with different "
"arguments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "M68K:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:86
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int cacheflush(unsigned long >I<addr>B<, int >I<scope>B<, int "
">I<cache>B<,>\n"
"B<               unsigned long >I<size>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "SH:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:91
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int cacheflush(unsigned long >I<addr>B<, unsigned long >I<size>B<, int "
">I<op>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "NDS32:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:96
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int cacheflush(unsigned int >I<start>B<, unsigned int >I<end>B<, int "
">I<cache>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:102
msgid ""
"On the above architectures, glibc does not provide a wrapper for this system "
"call; call it using B<syscall>(2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:102
#, no-wrap
msgid "GCC alternative"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:108
msgid ""
"Unless you need the finer grained control that this system call provides, "
"you probably want to use the GCC built-in function "
"B<__builtin___clear_cache>(), which provides a portable interface across "
"platforms supported by GCC and compatible compilers:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<void __builtin___clear_cache(void *>I<begin>B<, void *>I<end>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:118
msgid ""
"On platforms that don't require instruction cache flushes, "
"B<__builtin___clear_cache>()  has no effect."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:126
msgid ""
"I<Note>: On some GCC-compatible compilers, the prototype for this built-in "
"function uses I<char *> instead of I<void *> for the parameters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:132
msgid ""
"Historically, this system call was available on all MIPS UNIX variants "
"including RISC/os, IRIX, Ultrix, NetBSD, OpenBSD, and FreeBSD (and also on "
"some non-UNIX MIPS operating systems), so that the existence of this call in "
"MIPS operating systems is a de-facto standard."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:139
msgid ""
"Linux kernels older than Linux 2.6.11 ignore the I<addr> and I<nbytes> "
"arguments, making this function fairly expensive.  Therefore, the whole "
"cache is always flushed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cacheflush.2:146
msgid ""
"This function always behaves as if B<BCACHE> has been passed for the "
"I<cache> argument and does not do any error checking on the I<cache> "
"argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "cachestat"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "2025-06-28"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:8
msgid "cachestat - query the page cache statistics of a file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:11 build/C/man2/madvise.2:15
#: build/C/man2/mlock.2:16 build/C/man2/mmap.2:17 build/C/man2/mprotect.2:16
#: build/C/man2/msync.2:15 build/C/man3/posix_madvise.3:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<#include E<lt>sys/mman.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:14
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int cachestat(unsigned int >I<fd>B<, struct cachestat_range "
"*>I<cstat_range>B<,>\n"
"B<              struct cachestat *>I<cstat>B<, unsigned int >I<flags>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<struct cachestat_range {>\n"
"B<__u64 off;>\n"
"B<__u64 len;>\n"
"B<};>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:29
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<struct cachestat {>\n"
"B<__u64 nr_cache;>\n"
"B<__u64 nr_dirty;>\n"
"B<__u64 nr_writeback;>\n"
"B<__u64 nr_evicted;>\n"
"B<__u64 nr_recently_evicted;>\n"
"B<};>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:41
msgid ""
"B<cachestat()> queries the number of cached pages, dirty pages, pages marked "
"for writeback, evicted pages, and recently evicted pages in the byte range "
"specified by I<.off> and I<.len> in the B<cachestat_range> structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:46
msgid ""
"An evicted page is one that was previously in the page cache but has since "
"been evicted.  A page is considered recently evicted if its reentry into the "
"cache would indicate active usage under memory pressure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:52
msgid ""
"The results are returned in a B<cachestat> structure, pointed to by the "
"I<cstat> argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:67
msgid ""
"The I<.off> and I<.len> fields must be non-negative.  If "
"I<.len\\~E<gt>\\~0>, the queried range is [I<.off>,\\~I<.off+.len>].  If "
"I<len\\~==\\~0>, the range is from I<.off> to the end of the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:72
msgid "The I<flags> argument is reserved for future use and must be set to B<0>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:76
msgid "Currently, B<hugetlbfs> files are not supported."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:84
msgid ""
"On success, B<cachestat()> returns 0.  On error, -1 is returned, and "
"I<errno> is set to indicate the error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:91
msgid "I<cstat> or I<cstat_range> point to an invalid address."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:96
msgid "Invalid I<flags> value."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:96 build/C/man2/fallocate.2:307
#: build/C/man2/madvise.2:807 build/C/man2/mmap.2:515
#: build/C/man2/posix_fadvise.2:136 build/C/man3/posix_fallocate.3:57
#: build/C/man2/readahead.2:56 build/C/man2/sync_file_range.2:130
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<EBADF>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:99
msgid "Invalid file descriptor."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:99 build/C/man2/fallocate.2:419
#: build/C/man3/posix_fallocate.3:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<EOPNOTSUPP>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:104
msgid "The file descriptor refers to a B<hugetlbfs> file, which is unsupported."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:106 build/C/man2/fallocate.2:469
#: build/C/man2/mlock.2:308 build/C/man2/mmap2.2:76 build/C/man2/mprotect.2:267
#: build/C/man2/mremap.2:368 build/C/man2/readahead.2:73
#: build/C/man2/remap_file_pages.2:146 build/C/man2/subpage_prot.2:77
#: build/C/man2/sync_file_range.2:187 build/C/man2/memfd_create.2:208
#: build/C/man2/memfd_secret.2:135 build/C/man2/membarrier.2:286
#: build/C/man2/pkey_alloc.2:89
msgid "Linux."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:108
msgid "Linux 6.5."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:108 build/C/man3/malloc_usable_size.3:48
#: build/C/man3/memalign.3:53 build/C/man2/mmap.2:881
#: build/C/man3/posix_fallocate.3:141 build/C/man3/posix_memalign.3:94
#: build/C/man3/pvalloc.3:44
#, no-wrap
msgid "CAVEATS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/cachestat.2:112
msgid ""
"Note that the status of a page may change after B<cachestat()> retrieves it "
"but before the values are returned to the application; thus, the values may "
"be slightly outdated."
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "fallocate"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:9
msgid "fallocate - manipulate file space"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:16
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<#define _GNU_SOURCE>             /* See feature_test_macros(7) */\n"
"B<#include E<lt>fcntl.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int fallocate(int >I<fd>B<, int >I<mode>B<, off_t >I<offset>B<, off_t "
">I<size>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:25
msgid ""
"This is a nonportable, Linux-specific system call.  For the portable, "
"POSIX.1-specified method of ensuring that space is allocated for a file, see "
"B<posix_fallocate>(3)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:35
msgid ""
"B<fallocate>()  allows the caller to directly manipulate the allocated disk "
"space for the file referred to by I<fd> for the byte range starting at "
"I<offset> and continuing for I<size> bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:40
msgid ""
"The I<mode> argument determines the operation to be performed on the given "
"range.  Details of the supported operations are given in the subsections "
"below."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:40
#, no-wrap
msgid "Allocating disk space"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:63
msgid ""
"The default operation (i.e., I<mode> is zero) of B<fallocate>()  allocates "
"the disk space within the range specified by I<offset> and I<size>.  The "
"file size (as reported by B<stat>(2))  will be changed if I<offset>+I<size> "
"is greater than the file size.  Any subregion within the range specified by "
"I<offset> and I<size> that did not contain data before the call will be "
"initialized to zero.  This default behavior closely resembles the behavior "
"of the B<posix_fallocate>(3)  library function, and is intended as a method "
"of optimally implementing that function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:69
msgid ""
"After a successful call, subsequent writes into the range specified by "
"I<offset> and I<size> are guaranteed not to fail because of lack of disk "
"space."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:80
msgid ""
"If the B<FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE> flag is specified in I<mode>, the behavior of "
"the call is similar, but the file size will not be changed even if "
"I<offset>+I<size> is greater than the file size.  Preallocating zeroed "
"blocks beyond the end of the file in this manner is useful for optimizing "
"append workloads."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:90
msgid ""
"If the B<FALLOC_FL_UNSHARE_RANGE> flag is specified in I<mode>, shared file "
"data extents will be made private to the file to guarantee that a subsequent "
"write will not fail due to lack of space.  Typically, this will be done by "
"performing a copy-on-write operation on all shared data in the file.  This "
"flag may not be supported by all filesystems."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:94
msgid ""
"Because allocation is done in block size chunks, B<fallocate>()  may "
"allocate a larger range of disk space than was specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:94
#, no-wrap
msgid "Deallocating file space"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:109
msgid ""
"Specifying the B<FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE> flag (available since Linux 2.6.38) "
"in I<mode> deallocates space (i.e., creates a hole)  in the byte range "
"starting at I<offset> and continuing for I<size> bytes.  Within the "
"specified range, partial filesystem blocks are zeroed, and whole filesystem "
"blocks are removed from the file.  After a successful call, subsequent reads "
"from this range will return zeros."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:120
msgid ""
"The B<FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE> flag must be ORed with B<FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE> in "
"I<mode>; in other words, even when punching off the end of the file, the "
"file size (as reported by B<stat>(2))  does not change."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:125
msgid ""
"Not all filesystems support B<FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE>; if a filesystem doesn't "
"support the operation, an error is returned.  The operation is supported on "
"at least the following filesystems:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:127
msgid "XFS (since Linux 2.6.38)"
msgstr ""

#.  commit a4bb6b64e39abc0e41ca077725f2a72c868e7622
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:130
msgid "ext4 (since Linux 3.0)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:132
msgid "Btrfs (since Linux 3.7)"
msgstr ""

#.  commit 83e4fa9c16e4af7122e31be3eca5d57881d236fe
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:136
msgid "B<tmpfs>(5)  (since Linux 3.5)"
msgstr ""

#.  commit 4e56a6411fbce6f859566e17298114c2434391a4
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:140
msgid "B<gfs2>(5)  (since Linux 4.16)"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:140
#, no-wrap
msgid "Collapsing file space"
msgstr ""

#.  commit 00f5e61998dd17f5375d9dfc01331f104b83f841
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:160
msgid ""
"Specifying the B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> flag (available since Linux 3.15) "
"in I<mode> removes a byte range from a file, without leaving a hole.  The "
"byte range to be collapsed starts at I<offset> and continues for I<size> "
"bytes.  At the completion of the operation, the contents of the file "
"starting at the location I<offset+size> will be appended at the location "
"I<offset>, and the file will be I<size> bytes smaller."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:174
msgid ""
"A filesystem may place limitations on the granularity of the operation, in "
"order to ensure efficient implementation.  Typically, I<offset> and I<size> "
"must be a multiple of the filesystem logical block size, which varies "
"according to the filesystem type and configuration.  If a filesystem has "
"such a requirement, B<fallocate>()  fails with the error B<EINVAL> if this "
"requirement is violated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:183
msgid ""
"If the region specified by I<offset> plus I<size> reaches or passes the end "
"of file, an error is returned; instead, use B<ftruncate>(2)  to truncate a "
"file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:188
msgid ""
"No other flags may be specified in I<mode> in conjunction with "
"B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE>."
msgstr ""

#.  commit 9eb79482a97152930b113b51dff530aba9e28c8e
#.  commit e1d8fb88a64c1f8094b9f6c3b6d2d9e6719c970d
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:196
msgid ""
"As at Linux 3.15, B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> is supported by ext4 (only for "
"extent-based files)  and XFS."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:196
#, no-wrap
msgid "Zeroing file space"
msgstr ""

#.  commit 409332b65d3ed8cfa7a8030f1e9d52f372219642
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:212
msgid ""
"Specifying the B<FALLOC_FL_ZERO_RANGE> flag (available since Linux 3.15)  in "
"I<mode> zeros space in the byte range starting at I<offset> and continuing "
"for I<size> bytes.  Within the specified range, blocks are preallocated for "
"the regions that span the holes in the file.  After a successful call, "
"subsequent reads from this range will return zeros."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:218
msgid ""
"Zeroing is done within the filesystem preferably by converting the range "
"into unwritten extents.  This approach means that the specified range will "
"not be physically zeroed out on the device (except for partial blocks at the "
"either end of the range), and I/O is (otherwise) required only to update "
"metadata."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:230
msgid ""
"If the B<FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE> flag is additionally specified in I<mode>, the "
"behavior of the call is similar, but the file size will not be changed even "
"if I<offset>+I<size> is greater than the file size.  This behavior is the "
"same as when preallocating space with B<FALLOC_FL_KEEP_SIZE> specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:235
msgid ""
"Not all filesystems support B<FALLOC_FL_ZERO_RANGE>; if a filesystem doesn't "
"support the operation, an error is returned.  The operation is supported on "
"at least the following filesystems:"
msgstr ""

#.  commit 376ba313147b4172f3e8cf620b9fb591f3e8cdfa
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:238
msgid "XFS (since Linux 3.15)"
msgstr ""

#.  commit b8a8684502a0fc852afa0056c6bb2a9273f6fcc0
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:241
msgid "ext4, for extent-based files (since Linux 3.15)"
msgstr ""

#.  commit 30175628bf7f521e9ee31ac98fa6d6fe7441a556
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:244
msgid "SMB3 (since Linux 3.17)"
msgstr ""

#.  commit f27451f229966874a8793995b8e6b74326d125df
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:247
msgid "Btrfs (since Linux 4.16)"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:247
#, no-wrap
msgid "Increasing file space"
msgstr ""

#.  commit dd46c787788d5bf5b974729d43e4c405814a4c7d
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:270
msgid ""
"Specifying the B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE> flag (available since Linux 4.1)  "
"in I<mode> increases the file space by inserting a hole within the file size "
"without overwriting any existing data.  The hole will start at I<offset> and "
"continue for I<size> bytes.  When inserting the hole inside file, the "
"contents of the file starting at I<offset> will be shifted upward (i.e., to "
"a higher file offset) by I<size> bytes.  Inserting a hole inside a file "
"increases the file size by I<size> bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:284
msgid ""
"This mode has the same limitations as B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> regarding "
"the granularity of the operation.  If the granularity requirements are not "
"met, B<fallocate>()  fails with the error B<EINVAL>.  If the I<offset> is "
"equal to or greater than the end of file, an error is returned.  For such "
"operations (i.e., inserting a hole at the end of file), B<ftruncate>(2)  "
"should be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:289
msgid ""
"No other flags may be specified in I<mode> in conjunction with "
"B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE>."
msgstr ""

#.  commit a904b1ca5751faf5ece8600e18cd3b674afcca1b
#.  commit 331573febb6a224bc50322e3670da326cb7f4cfc
#.  f2fs also has support since Linux 4.2
#.      commit f62185d0e283e9d311e3ac1020f159d95f0aab39
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:299
msgid ""
"B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE> requires filesystem support.  Filesystems that "
"support this operation include XFS (since Linux 4.1)  and ext4 (since Linux "
"4.2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:306
msgid ""
"On success, B<fallocate>()  returns zero.  On error, -1 is returned and "
"I<errno> is set to indicate the error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:311 build/C/man3/posix_fallocate.3:61
msgid "I<fd> is not a valid file descriptor, or is not opened for writing."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:311 build/C/man2/fallocate.2:315
#: build/C/man3/posix_fallocate.3:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<EFBIG>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:315
msgid "I<offset>+I<size> exceeds the maximum file size."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:323
msgid ""
"I<mode> is B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE>, and the current file size+I<len> "
"exceeds the maximum file size."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:323 build/C/man3/posix_fallocate.3:65
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<EINTR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:328
msgid "A signal was caught during execution; see B<signal>(7)."
msgstr ""

#.  FIXME . (raise a kernel bug) Probably the size==0 case should be
#.  a no-op, rather than an error.  That would be consistent with
#.  similar APIs for the size==0 case.
#.  See "Re: [PATCH] fallocate.2: add FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE flag definition"
#.  21 Sep 2012
#.  http://thread.gmane.org/gmane.linux.file-systems/48331/focus=1193526
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:340
msgid "I<offset> was less than 0, or I<size> was less than or equal to 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:350
msgid ""
"I<mode> is B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> and the range specified by I<offset> "
"plus I<size> reaches or passes the end of the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:358
msgid ""
"I<mode> is B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE> and the range specified by I<offset> "
"reaches or passes the end of the file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:370
msgid ""
"I<mode> is B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> or B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE>, but "
"either I<offset> or I<size> is not a multiple of the filesystem block size."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fallocate.2:382
msgid ""
"I<mode> contains one of B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> or "
"B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE> and also other flags; no other flags are permitted "
"with B<FALLOC_FL_COLLAPSE_RANGE> or B<FALLOC_FL_INSERT_RANGE>."
msgstr ""

#.  There was an inconsistency in Linux 3.15-rc1, that should be resolved so that all
#.  filesystems use this error for this case.  (Tytso says ex4 will change.)
